<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mularaja II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1175–<a href="/years/1178">1178</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mularaja II was a child king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, ruling from about 1175 to 1178 under the regency of his mother Naikidevi. In 1178 the Chaulukya army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kasahrada">Battle of Kasahrada</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a>, one of the few defeats suffered by the Ghurid ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mularaja, known as Bala Mularaja, the Child Mularaja, succeeded his father Ajayapala on the Chaulukya throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilapataka</a> (modern Patan) while still a young child. His mother Naikidevi was the daughter of a ruler named Paramardin, identified by one theory with the Goa Kadamba king Shivachitta Paramadideva and by another with the Chandela king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramardi">Paramardi</a>. The 14th-century chronicler Merutunga says that Naikidevi fought the Muslims at Gadararaghatta, which appears to be the Kasahrada where the Chaulukya army defeated the Ghurids in 1178, when the king was thirteen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Paramara kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> had come under Chaulukya control under Mularaja's predecessors. During his reign a severe famine struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and the Paramara king Vindhyavarman took the chance to try to win Malwa back. According to the Surathotsava, written by the poet Someshvara, his father Kumara, the royal priest, persuaded the king to give his subjects a tax break during the famine and also commanded the army against Vindhyavarman.</p><p>Someshvara says Kumara defeated the Paramara king, destroyed his town of Gogasthana, sank a well where the Paramara palace had stood and plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, giving the wealth at Gaya during a shraddha ceremony; he also defeated a mlechchha army near Rajnisara. Vindhyavarman nonetheless regained Malwa in the end. R. C. Majumdar held that this happened during Mularaja's reign, while A. K. Majumdar believed Malwa stayed under Chaulukya control until after it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mularaja died at a very young age in 1178, the year of the victory over the Ghurids, and was succeeded by his brother Bhima II. The regency of his mother Naikidevi, the famine, the tax relief and the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> are known mainly from Merutunga and from the Surathotsava Mahakavya of Someshvara, the son of Kumara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mularaja II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mularaja II</b> defeated Muhammad of Ghor in 1178, but Qutb al-Din Aibak sacked Anahilavada in 1197.</blockquote><a href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
